 Steele-John","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10901","person_slug":"jordon-steele-john","vote":"no"},{"name":"Josh Dolega","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_11054","person_slug":"josh-dolega","vote":"no"},{"name":"Larissa Waters","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10764","person_slug":"larissa-waters","vote":"no"},{"name":"Lisa Darmanin","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_11021","person_slug":"lisa-darmanin","vote":"no"},{"name":"Malarndirri McCarthy","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10855","person_slug":"malarndirri-mccarthy","vote":"no"},{"name":"Malcolm Roberts","party":"Pauline Hanson's One Nation Party","person_id":"tvfy_10856","person_slug":"malcolm-roberts","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Marielle Smith","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10953","person_slug":"marielle-smith","vote":"no"},{"name":"Matt O'Sullivan","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10958","person_slug":"matt-osullivan","vote":"no"},{"name":"Mehreen Faruqi","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10912","person_slug":"mehreen-faruqi","vote":"no"},{"name":"Michelle Ananda-Rajah","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10983","person_slug":"michelle-ananda-rajah","vote":"no"},{"name":"Nick McKim","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10841","person_slug":"nick-mckim","vote":"no"},{"name":"Nita Green","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10950","person_slug":"nita-green","vote":"no"},{"name":"Pauline Hanson","party":"Pauline Hanson's One Nation Party","person_id":"tvfy_10280","person_slug":"pauline-hanson","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Penny Allman-Payne","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_11002","person_slug":"penny-allman-payne","vote":"no"},{"name":"Raff Ciccone","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10915","person_slug":"raff-ciccone","vote":"no"},{"name":"Ralph Babet","party":"United Australia Party","person_id":"tvfy_11003","person_slug":"ralph-babet","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Richard Dowling","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_11056","person_slug":"richard-dowling","vote":"no"},{"name":"Sarah Hanson-Young","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10711","person_slug":"sarah-hanson-young","vote":"no"},{"name":"Sean Bell","party":"Pauline Hanson's One Nation Party","person_id":"tvfy_11062","person_slug":"sean-bell","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Steph Hodgins-May","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_11020","person_slug":"steph-hodgins-may","vote":"no"},{"name":"Tim Ayres","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10946","person_slug":"tim-ayres","vote":"no"},{"name":"Tony Sheldon","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10944","person_slug":"tony-sheldon","vote":"no"},{"name":"Tyron Whitten","party":"Pauline Hanson's One Nation Party","person_id":"tvfy_11060","person_slug":"tyron-whitten","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Vanessa Bleyer","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_11065","person_slug":"vanessa-bleyer","vote":"no"},{"name":"Varun Ghosh","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_11017","person_slug":"varun-ghosh","vote":"no"}],"name":"Bills — A New Tax System (Family Assistance) Amendment (No Jab No Pay Repeal) Bill 2025; Second Reading","noes":34,"question":"Alex Antic . The A New Tax System (Family Assistance) Amendment (No Jab No Pay Repeal) Bill 2025 is a bill which stands for the principle of conscientious objection. The bill seeks to restore freedom of choice for parents who, for personal, philosophical, religious or medical reasons, elect not to have a child receive a vaccine from the standard vaccine schedule. It would achieve this by repealing the changes made to the Social Services Legislation Amendment (No Jab, No Pay) Bill 2015. In 2015, the so-called no-jab no-pay amendment made certain family assistance benefits conditional on meeting vaccine and vaccination requirements by amending the A New Tax System (Family Assistance) Act 1999. Access to the childcare benefit, childcare rebate and family tax benefit part A became conditional on a child meeting all the immunisation requirements. Childcare benefit and childcare rebate have since been replaced by the child care subsidy. No-jab no-pay removed the conscientious objection exemption regarding being eligible for these forms of government assistance for families, meaning that, if parents want to receive those benefits, their child's vaccination schedule must be fully up to date. Now, the removal of the conscientious objection against vaccination to receive these benefits is, in my view, needlessly cruel and coercive as a measure designed to financially pressure parents, many of whom are of a low-socioeconomic background, into having their children vaccinated, even if they hold personal, philosophical, religious or indeed medical objections to doing so. A decision to vaccinate a child, wherever such reasons exist, should be a personal decision. Contrary to what our modern healthcare apparatus seems to think, legitimate reasons for conscientious objections exist. Indeed, that was the consensus in 1999, with the A New Tax System (Family Assistance) Act 1999, so it wasn't actually all that long ago that the right to conscientious objection was considered important ","result":"negative","slug":"division-federal-senate-10789","source_url":"https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/divisions/senate/2026-09-16/1"}
